-- title:
--   Abel's theorem: sufficiency of the period condition
-- statement:
--   Let $F$ be a field equipped with a $\mathbb{C}$-algebra structure, and assume $F$ contains an element $x$ transcendental over $\mathbb{C}$ such that $F$ is finite-dimensional over $\mathbb{C}(x)$ (the intermediate field generated by $x$). Assume `IsCurveOver ℂ F`: every nonzero $f \in F$ has a divisor of degree zero, each place of $F$ over $\mathbb{C}$ has residue field finite over $\mathbb{C}$, and $\Omega[F/\mathbb{C}]$ is free of rank one over $F$; assume `HasCanonicalDivisor`, i.e. every nonzero $\omega \in \Omega[F/\mathbb{C}]$ has finitely many places with $\mathrm{ord}_v^{\mathrm{diff}}(\omega) \neq 0$, so that these orders form a divisor. Assume the place set $\mathrm{Place}(\mathbb{C},F)$ — places being nontrivial valuation subrings containing $\mathbb{C}$ that are principal ideal rings — carries a topology and $\mathbb{C}$-charted analytic manifold structure making it compact, Hausdorff and connected, and assume the compatibility hypothesis $hF$: for every $f \neq 0$ and every place $v$, the function $z \mapsto \mathrm{evalAt}_{(\mathrm{extChartAt}\,v)^{-1}(z)}(f)$ is meromorphic at the chart image of $v$ with meromorphic order exactly $\mathrm{ord}_v(f)$. Fix $n$, a $\mathbb{C}$-basis $b$ of the submodule `regularDifferentials ℂ F` of differentials that are locally $f \cdot \mathrm{dCoord}_v$ with $f$ in the valuation ring at $v$, a base place $P_0$, and a divisor $D$ (a finitely supported $\mathbb{Z}$-valued function on places) with $\deg D = 0$. If the Abel–Jacobi vector $\mathrm{abelJacobiDiv}(b, P_0)(D)$, the additive extension to divisors of $P \mapsto (\int_{P_0}^{P} b_i)_i$ computed along a chosen path, lies in the lattice spanned over $\mathbb{Z}$ by the period vectors $(\int_\gamma b_i)_i$ of loops $\gamma$, then $D$ is principal: there is $f \in F$, $f \neq 0$, with $D(v) = \mathrm{ord}_v(f)$ for every place $v$.
--
--   This is the substantial half of Abel's theorem for a compact Riemann surface: vanishing of the Abel–Jacobi image modulo periods suffices for a degree-zero divisor to be the divisor of a function. It underlies the identification of $\mathrm{Pic}^0$ with a quotient of $\mathbb{C}^n$ by the period lattice and the divisibility statements for differences of points used downstream.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_AlgebraicCurve_Divisor_isPrincipal_of_abelJacobiDiv_mem_pathPeriodLattice.lean

